### The parent cannot get a break because the child keeps moving, talking or demanding their attention.

6 of 190 posts.

Parents describe children who rarely stop moving, talking, interrupting or demanding play and bodily contact, including during classes or quiet time. The children are mainly three or nearly four, with one nearly three; parents report disrupted sleep, overstimulation, limited ability to attend to another child and difficulty completing tasks. Attempts include outdoor play, gymnastics or dance, limiting TV, sticker charts, quiet time and explanations, with parents asking when independent play or a calmer phase will arrive.

- "She is constantly moving. Jumping up and down, bouncing off of the walls (literally), cartwheeling and summer-salting." [ar063-0022]
- "if you dare to say another sentence to another human its met with a "MUMMY PLAY WITH ME, PLAY WITH ME, PLAY!!!!!!!!!!"" [ar065-0059]
- "Nothing keeps her attention and nothing seems to work. It’s like she just zones me out and it feels like my kid is the only kid who ever acts up." [ar007-0088]
- "I feel like I can't breathe sometimes because I get a single hour to myself during the day and he just can't let me have it." [ar004-0099]
- "i feel like a shell of a human and i’ve lately even considered asking my husband if we can just coparent because it’d be so much easier knowing i’d have a break." [ar075-0177]

**But:** A parent seeking relief also says they intend to keep giving the child frequent role play and individual attention: "do my very best to do even a tiny bit of role play every time he asks, (it's not something I am going to change)" [ar065-0063]

Posts: 1tk553t 1uevgcb 1uhdy5p 1v7l8p7 1vr0huc 1wmv9hz

### The parent wants meals to stop becoming battles over sitting, eating or throwing food.

4 of 190 posts.

Parents of children nearly three to three-and-a-half want meals without constant prompting, leaving the table, food throwing or damage to the room. Some discuss the same sitting difficulty with preschool; others disagree about ending dinner or fear the emotional effect of taking food away. Repeated redirection, food removal and proposed cleanup or bedtime consequences do not supply a shared mealtime solution here.

- "No one in the family is relaxed during meals, the “come back for another bite” takes over any conversations and it’s at an infuriating point for everyone." [ar073-0052]
- "We eat dinner together as a family at the dining table each day, but we’re also constantly directing him back to the table. Feeling exhausted and defeated." [ar055-0001]
- "things like smearing sauces across the countertop and flinging applesauce on the walls on purpose has become a nightly event." [ar066-0035]
- "It weighs on her conscience that he'll possibly remember Mommy constantly taking food from him." [ar051-0099]

**But:** The food-throwing parent identifies several possible situations, rather than treating every throw as the same act: "This is usually done when he's unexcited about the food provided, he's bored/tired, wants attention, or is just simply finished eating his meal." [ar051-0098]

Posts: 1se8apv 1tg16x2 1to7rb7 1xdjwda

### The parent worries about copied or hurtful words and wants to teach kinder speech.

6 of 190 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds ask about copied rude or racial language, a racial rejection at a market, and repeated teasing about a postpartum tummy. They describe embarrassment, hurt, fear of public assumptions or concern about raising a bully, and try explanations, ending playdates, ignoring provocative words or changing music. The reports distinguish what a child says from the parent's uncertainty about its meaning or source.

- "“everyone looks so lovely! Except the black girls. I don’t like the black girls”" [ar021-0056]
- "Now my son has been chanting oba when ever he sees a black person." [ar012-0144]
- "The biggest issue is the N-word. Nobody in our household uses that word at all. He heard it a few times in rap music playing in my husband’s car, and now he randomly says it." [ar081-0127]
- "My toddler has started telling us “ Go away find a new home!” “ I don’t like you find a new home “ “ I no like you. Leave “." [ar046-0063]
- "I’m genuinely confused and (okay, being human here) a little hurt that she continues to ask me." [ar073-0076]

**But:** One parent explicitly says the toddler does not understand the offensive words; the interpretation is the parent's: "He obviously has absolutely no idea what either word means. He’ll just randomly say them because I think he remembers the sound and knows it gets a reaction." [ar081-0128]

Posts: 1trimuj 1utgcsi 1vfk74y 1vpfmbu 1w73xo7 1weit3v

### The parent wants to stop a repetitive habit that damages skin, nails or clothing.

4 of 190 posts.

These posts concern nail biting that hurts fingers, a five-month-old scratching her thighs, self-biting after parents block biting them, and a two-and-a-half-year-old picking apart a diaper's outer layer. Parents seek ways to prevent damage or meet the apparent sensory or calming need, using filing, skin care, bitter or coloured polish, socks on a damaged toy and calm body-safety talk. Explanations about anxiety, control or sensory motives are the parents' interpretations, not diagnoses established by these posts.

- "My 3 year old has started biting her nails and by that I mean she literally has like half of her nail bed left bc she bites the rest off." [ar057-0039]
- "My 5mo obsessively scratches her inner thighs whenever they’re accessible." [ar050-0022]
- "The part that I’m not sure how to handle is him biting himself. He does it hard enough to leave an impression." [ar076-0145]
- "Her daycare teacher had now let us k ow that she does it at nap, and the other kids are finding pieces and trying to eat them." [ar040-0089]

**But:** The nail-biting parent reports early interest in a replacement action: "She seems to like the idea and even saw her trying it at one point without me even asking 😲" [ar057-0047]

Posts: 1tsl3du 1udipbd 1vdxcn1 1wolr28

## Pattern 3, share 04: near posts

### The parent keeps repeating instructions, but the child refuses and ordinary routines become power struggles.

9 of 21 posts.

These nine posts focus on sleep: two- and three-year-olds resist bedtime, demand a parent nearby, repeatedly get up or wake screaming in the night. Parents describe consistent routines, sleep training, warnings, punishment, rewards, co-sleeping, lights, sensory input, blankets or comfort objects, often while another baby is arriving or has arrived. They want an end to prolonged bedtime or repeated night waking without establishing an unwanted sleeping arrangement.

- "She would fall asleep standing up and fell a few times." [ar006-0006]
- "We’ve tried ignoring it, giving him warnings, taking away his favorite toys, spankings" [ar081-0028]
- "he falls asleep immediately in our bed but it makes it very uncomfortable." [ar022-0077]
- "Something works for a while, we make progress, and then eventually it stops working or the problem morphs into something else." [ar037-0135]
- "She’ll beg for the other parent or cry and scream until she throws up." [ar039-0131]
- "She tosses any stuffy we give her. We are hardcore struggling mentally here." [ar054-0131]

**But:** The sleep problems can be concentrated at bedtime, with overnight sleep otherwise intact: "She’s been sleeping in her own bed for about a year now, and almost always sleeps through the night." [ar039-0129]

Posts: 1sd9syz 1ska4rr 1u2kxyo 1uwzexb 1vtl8a1 1w5u089 1wsq2xd 1x9ol3q 1xek739

### The parent wants to change their own reactions while keeping limits and connection.

1 of 21 posts.

One parent of a two-year-old and a ten-week-old describes the older child's jealousy and aggression despite substantial individual attention. The parent stays outwardly calm but wants to change angry, resentful feelings and help a speech-delayed toddler who cannot yet explain emotions. They have ended activities after aggression and offered a stuffed toy to squeeze, and worry about coping after the husband's leave ends.

- "Sometimes if we tell her no about something, sometimes instead of hitting us, she will run of to the baby instead and try to hit him." [ar011-0015]
- "We've tried handing her a stuffy and telling her to squeeze that when she starts to feel angry." [ar011-0016]
- "I'm finding myself feeling angry and resentful towards her when she is mean to the baby." [ar011-0017]

**But:** The parent distinguishes internal distress from an outward loss of control: "I don't yell at her or anything and I am able to keep outwardly calm, but I don't like how I feel." [ar011-0017]

Posts: 1syicmr

### The parent wants room for their own needs and questions whether they can bear more demands from children.

5 of 21 posts.

Parents describe regretful thoughts, depletion, comfort eating, dread of solo childcare or uncertainty about another baby. Most current young children are two or three, with one account including a four-year-old and another looking back on toddler years from an older family stage. They want validation or practical room for themselves through work, daycare, rest, therapy or managing eating; love and affection coexist with wishing the demands would ease.

- "I've developed a negative association and I'm starting to dread those days when they roll around, and upon waking I'm automatically in a bad mood." [ar070-0078]
- "I'm having moments where I wish I didn't have kids at all and wasn't carrying another one." [ar012-0035]
- "La nourriture est du coup devenue mon doudou. Quand je suis fatiguée, stressée ou que j'ai besoin de réconfort, je mange." [ar036-0043]
- "When I don’t see her, I don’t miss her for example. I rather cook and clean than play with her." [ar015-0095]
- "I go to therapy once every two weeks (can’t afford more)." [ar015-0096]
- "Now we can go to the grocery store together or when we indulge out to eat and enjoy that. Will a baby in tow ruin those experiences?" [ar048-0099]

**But:** The ambivalence is not a denial of affection or of good periods: "We co-sleep, I'm still breastfeeding her once at bedtime, we are a very affectionate and close family overall." [ar070-0077]

Posts: 1t935co 1ukf1r9 1vcpm2n 1w8lqum 1wkrexv

### The parent wants to stop the child hurting or ignoring the boundaries of other children.

1 of 21 posts.

One parent asks whether a kindergarten game amounts to enabling bullying and how to talk about it or find a suitable book. The child's exact age is unknown in the share; the concern comes from the son's account of targeting a socially awkward child who may have developmental difficulties. The parent has already discussed being the friend of a bully and wants to know whether the other child is actually participating willingly.

- "My son said his job is to find the other kid so his friend can go and “tackle” him." [ar067-0049]
- "But I’m concerned that they’re basically targeting this kid for being different." [ar067-0050]
- "Any advice on good conversation starters to help him see if the other kid is actually playing along or not?" [ar067-0051]

**But:** The parent has no corroborating complaint from the school or another parent: "This is all coming from my son’s account. The school hasn’t said there are any issues, and no parents have reached out." [ar067-0050]

Posts: 1tnnpte

### The poster questions why schools punish defiance when it may be a response to trauma.

1 of 21 posts.

This is a discussion of school punishment, trauma and authority, with no identified child's age and a speaker recorded as other. The poster asks why school discipline differs from an understanding of defiance as a trauma response. The post attributes one passage to Google, and the share's note also names Psychology Today; authorship of the Google passage, including whether it is AI-written, is unspecified.

- "However, rather than willful disobedience, this rebellion is a trauma response." [ar035-0081]
- "Why do schools still punish disobedience? Heck I've even run into therapist who say school is about following authority." [ar035-0082]
- "The funny thing is I got this feeling that there are parents out there who are purposely strict with their kids because they don't want them to succeed." [ar035-0084]

**But:** nothing found against it.

Posts: 1ue4g30

### The parent wants meals to stop becoming battles over sitting, eating or throwing food.

3 of 21 posts.

Three parents describe food refusal or restricted eating alongside tantrums, poor mood or prolonged prompting, in children aged about two-and-a-quarter to nearly four. They try low-pressure feeding, safe foods, autonomy, withholding snacks or repeated bite prompts, and one reports a clinician's concern about low weight. Parents want enough food and calmer meals, with uncertainty about whether behaviour, hunger or the parent-child setting drives the difficulty.

- "I’ve tried many different approaches: no pressure, one safe food, 2 safe foods etc." [ar037-0098]
- "my husband and I ended up taking turns in the other room with him so we could eat." [ar037-0099]
- "The real problem with this is that he then turns into a monster, throwing fits, whining incessantly, and just generally being a terror." [ar011-0039]
- "There have been times we've sat at the table for up to an hour working on a single uncrustable sandwich." [ar026-0065]
- "at his last pediatrician appointment, they said he was underweight." [ar026-0069]

**But:** The refusal is not consistent across caregivers in one account: "See, when he's at his grandparents' house, he eats just fine." [ar026-0067]

Posts: 1uetu4n 1uhxn1g 1wqch9w

### The parent wants adults to protect a child from repeated harm by other children.

1 of 21 posts.

One parent considers how to act on a son's repeated account of a friend being physically and emotionally bullied. The son is entering sixth grade and the friend is still in elementary school; the friend's exact age is unknown, and academic and motor difficulties are reported second-hand. The parent weighs contacting the teacher, contacting the mother or encouraging disclosure through the son, while worrying about surprising the mother or creating conflict.

- "Based on what he tells me, this child is being bullied quite seriously at school, both physically and emotionally." [ar062-0090]
- "if another student pushes him, he might say that he fell." [ar062-0091]
- "I'm hesitant to get involved in a situation that doesn't directly concern my own child." [ar062-0096]
- "I really don't want to find myself in the middle of conflicts between parents." [ar062-0097]

**But:** The parent explicitly limits the certainty of the account: "I should make it clear that I obviously don't know the whole story, and I haven't witnessed any of these incidents myself. Everything I know comes from what my son has told me." [ar062-0092]

