### The parent wants to leave home, move between places and get back safely without a battle.

19 of 189 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe shopping, restaurants, gatherings, playground departures, daycare pickup and getting into a car seat becoming battles. Some are trying to keep a runner away from roads or strangers, sometimes while also caring for a baby; others dread being stared at or have stopped going out. They want necessary errands and enjoyable family experiences to remain possible, and report trying warnings, snacks, distractions, timers, waiting and physical removal.

- "I love my son more than anything and I just want to give him a normal childhood with memories and fun. I’ve turned down countless fun days this summer beach, water parks, festivals etc. because I feel like I can’t take him anywhere because of his behavior. To be completely honest I’m embarrassed and humiliated by his behavior. I feel like his behavior makes me look like a terrible mother and makes him look like a bad kid. Deep down I know he is a sweet, caring boy.  I just don’t know what gets into him some times." [ar048-0096]
- "I can barely go grocery shopping or into any store because my toddler completely loses it. She screams, fights to get out of the stroller/cart, grabs everything within reach, has ripped packaging, and has even broken things in stores before. She doesn’t care about toys as distractions, doesn’t watch shows on my phone, and if I let her walk, she immediately runs off everywhere. I physically can’t chase her around while carrying or caring for my infant." [ar039-0061]
- "[…] My toddler used to walk by side be so well and followed instructions on when to stop and wait for me. However, overnight she’s decided she wants to run and finds it funny. […]" [ar032-0043]
- "My son left our front door open and within minutes she’d left our house and ran to the neighbors. I almost called the police but they found her.  It was one of the worst moments of my life." [ar060-0067]
- "It started with her wanting to buckle her own seat belt. She wants her independence and wants to be able to do this herself, but we always have to take over at some point because she's unable to. […]" [ar071-0035]
- "[…] today my toddler refused to get in the car seat for one hour. one hour I sat at the daycare parking lot. […]" [ar058-0085]
- "I feel so isolated watching other toddlers just exist normally in public while I’m carrying a screaming child back to the car trying not to cry myself. Family members make comments. People stare. Everyone thinks kids should “just adjust,” but nobody talks about how mentally exhausting this is for the parent." [ar002-0110]
- "[…] Talking about what’s going on doesn’t help. I still try though. My husband and I had a miserable time. The thing is this is the kind of thing we want to do as family. We want our child with us. […]" [ar030-0097]

**But:** Difficulty can depend on the environment rather than every outing. "he is a great kid and extremely well behaved, no pulling throwing kicking none of that. He absolutely hates social gatherings. Infact when he is in a clean and less noisy set up he seems to do okay. And even when shopping." [ar002-0113]

