### The parent wants to protect siblings and help the child cope with sharing family life.

17 of 189 posts.

With focal children aged two to three-and-a-half, parents describe tantrums, clinginess, roughness and demands after a baby arrives, and conflict between established siblings. They try individual attention, modelling gentle touch, involving the toddler, separation and consequences, while struggling to keep everyone safe without making the older child feel rejected. Rough affection is sometimes described as unintentional, but other accounts describe repeated attacks; one post instead concerns a two-year-old frightened by an aggressive seven-year-old stepsibling.

- "STM here with a 2.5yo and 8wo. Really struggling with the toddler acting out when the baby is crying, causing chaos and generally doing whatever he knows is naughty. An example is today at bathtime - baby starts screaming and so he starts pouring and throwing water out of the bath. Thankfully my partner heard and came to help but it was 0-100 in terms of stress level and once it’s all settled I just feel so angry and frazzled and like I lost all control." [ar018-0001]
- "Like idk how to explain it but for being two she is SOOOO strong and almost impossible to redirect. She's broken my nose and gave me several bruises. She never did it on purpose but freaked out about being redirected and I was just unlucky that I was the one trying to redirect. When she is getting a diaper change or getting dressed when she doesn't want to and we have to she has kicked and thrashed enough to cause injury to my fiance and myself.  She always says sorry and tries to hug after." [ar008-0015]
- "[…] She used to be very attached to me before the baby came so I make sure I spend enough one on one time with her. Nothing seems to be working. […]" [ar024-0009]
- "[…] Ive tried using a timer but he likes the timer so he will purposely hit or kick to get to use it. […]" [ar038-0042]
- "We have tried ALL of the above, NOTHING WORKS. My 5 year old is rightfully upset that we haven’t been able to stop him. If I see any sign of conflict I’ll immediately grab him now but it’s not always possible to see it coming. It happens so quick." [ar003-0086]
- "After the baby was born 4.5 months ago she just regressed almost instantly. She became so angry and sad so often. She started with baby talk and just doing the opposite than what she is allowed to do all the time. She loves her brother and will therefore try to cuddle him aaaaall the time, she doesn’t let him be. And she is obviously jealous." [ar013-0124]
- "[…] Now she has started screaming in our 2 year olds face, scaring her to the point where she has peed herself. 7 year old will not sleep in the night and today woke our 2 year old up due to her screaming at the top of her lungs at 3am. My 2 year old was freaked out, crying & saying that it has scared her." [ar074-0119]
- "[…] Every SINGLE time he plays with his 2yo brother, he ends up getting too rough and hurting the little one. If they're playing chase, he gets too close and accidentally pushes brother to the floor. If they're wrestling, he takes it too far and ends up squishing/hurting brother. If they're jumping on the couch, he falls on brother. They cannot play together for more than a minute without the little one getting hurt." [ar005-0093]

**But:** Changed behaviour does not always mean hostility toward the baby. "My son is 2.5 years old and we brought home his baby brother last week. He's doing great with interacting with his brother and being gentle and sweet so that part has been good." [ar031-0055]

