### The parent is overwhelmed by the behaviour and wants to stop losing patience or resenting family life.

11 of 189 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe anger, exhaustion, guilt, resentment and sometimes regret about parenthood while still expressing love for the child. Several recount yelling, threats, physical retaliation or punishment, then want a way to respond differently or repair things; others want relief and an honest account of whether family life improves. Sleep loss, working while caring for a child, pregnancy, cancer recovery, lack of breaks and the destruction of a valued purchase appear in these accounts.

- "Part of me feels like I made a huge mistake having her, but the other half loves her endlessly and is so excited to raise her." [ar063-0049]
- "I just feel at the end of my tether, I had postnatal depression in the beginning and I can feel the depression creeping back in, I feel so trapped like this is never ending, she’s also such a daddy’s girl and prefers him so much to me, he is way more fun and better at parenting than I am tbh so I understand why she prefers him. I’m just so fed up and feel like I’ve honestly ruined my life." [ar044-0070]
- "I’m not sleeping, I’m spending all day trying to correct her behavior which puts me in a bad mood for my other kids, and I just am at a point that I can’t stand her." [ar051-0071]
- "[…] I smack his butt to try and stop him from going bizurk, then he crys and I feel horrible. […]" [ar052-0011]
- "The 3 year old came up to me, expressed his anger and poked me in the eye. Something that obviously shocked me that I lightly poked him back on his eye. No damage done (other than wrongfully teaching him, encouraging him to fight)" [ar055-0096]
- "Every time I raise my voice I feel horrible afterwards. I don’t want my sweet, loving son to remember me as the mom that was always yelling but I also don’t want to fall into permissive parenting either. […]" [ar061-0092]
- "I’m currently in the trenches of the trying 3’s and I’m so burned out. It doesn’t help that I’m recovering from a year of battling breast cancer and my nervous system is shot. I’m so quick to get triggered by my daughter’s whining and she’s currently in the stage where she saves all her poor behavior for me. I get the worst version of her and it’s so frustrating. I feel incredibly guilty for not enjoying this stage of her life. I don’t enjoy playing with her, I’m exhausted because she’s been waking up every night at various times screaming due to nightmares (I think) and my marriage isn’t in the best state so I feel like everything is falling apart." [ar023-0155]
- "I’m so through. I am a single mom doing this by myself, I never buy things for me and this was my first big purchase after she was born and she di this. She put a puzzle piece in it and slammed it closed. I want to throw up. Literally $1000 down the drain. […]" [ar055-0119]

**But:** There are also attempts to reconnect and remembered strategies, including a child reminding the adult. "[…] Finally when my 3yo was in bed and we both said our tearful apologies, she said “oh we should have done our list!” I didn’t even get it at first, but she was saying we should have done our silly routine instead of getting so worked up and breaking so many of our own rules." [ar032-0046]

