# Digest 04: pattern 3, Hard behaviour

Share: baby-development/_work/2026-11/digests-age-reread/share-04.md. Yes posts: 190. Near posts: 21.

## Pattern 3, share 04: yes posts

### The parent keeps repeating instructions, but the child refuses and ordinary routines become power struggles.

30 of 190 posts.

These parents describe repeated refusals across dressing, leaving daycare, cleaning up, play and bedtime; most children are two or three, including several nearly four. Choices, explanations, toy removal, timeouts and spanking are reported as ineffective or short-lived, and some parents ask for exact scripts or a course they can watch. Comparisons with more cooperative siblings coexist with accounts of a recent change in a previously cooperative child.

- "He is never following my rules without some whining, I have to repeat everything 10 times, and each time he makes up a distraction that he needs to do before proceeding." [ar003-0001]
- "It doesn’t even matter if it’s something fun or positive! It’s like a switch is flipped and it’s automatic pushback." [ar005-0002]
- "Today we took away multiple toys and went to bed without dinner or reading books. She doesn’t care." [ar073-0004]
- "I do not gentle parent. I believe in time outs, putting them in the corner, taking toys, having talks about why certain behavior is wrong, and yes, even spankings. None of it seems to matter with her." [ar071-0029]
- "Meanwhile my 3 year old is totally unbothered about not going to the park." [ar007-0090]
- "I have made it into play “who can clean up the fastest”, I’ve done timers, I’ve done consequences, I’ve taken things away, time out, forcing to clean up by moving his hands (he laughs)." [ar058-0050]
- "Looking for something that will actually teach me exactly what to say or do (like, almost scripts), not just general concepts." [ar002-0131]

**But:** One parent reports that a choice did produce cleanup, although it delayed bed: "We ​ were up a little late, but boy it looks tidy in here for a change!" [ar011-0106]

