### The parent wants to change their own reactions while keeping limits and connection.

13 of 190 posts.

Parents ask how to manage their own anger, guilt or uncertainty about a response while still addressing the child's behaviour. The situations include withholding bedtime play after hitting, leaving a gathering abruptly, struggling to comfort a child after correction, and finding it unsafe to step away from two fighting toddlers. Children are mainly two and three; one aunt describes a two- and four-year-old, while a single mother names the temptation to return to substances for relief.

- "I don't even know that she made the connection to why, but it felt like I was giving in to treat it like normal." [ar008-0003]
- "I just don’t want to yell. I want to be his safe space." [ar052-0009]
- "Now I am trying to hold back tears of frustration mostly at myself for now managing the stress better. I’m just tired y’all" [ar021-0087]
- "My worst memories are of my Dad getting in my face, and I don't want that to happen to my little girl. I just don't know how to do anything else, and I hope someone here does." [ar057-0112]
- "I used to have issues with substances which is one of the many bad habits I overcame in my journey of parenthood so I’m frightened to fall into that again, but I’d be lying if I didn’t say I was tempted to have a nightly ritual of doing something to take the edge off!" [ar052-0063]
- "I don’t want to be mad all the time, I don’t want them to remember me that way,  but I’m only human, and I can’t always just “step away.”" [ar068-0110]

**But:** The aunt reports improvement from a no-yelling challenge, including whispering, identifying triggers and connecting before correction: "But our home is much calmer now, and I feel far more in control of my reactions than I did before" [ar016-0048]

Posts: 1sax2t0 1sbkso2 1tijpmp 1tqkm56 1uaa71j 1uc85hr 1upajf6 1w1kzdm 1wdmsi7 1x0pvyf 1x4mwnh 1x5wgj1 1x8cgs6

### The parent wants to protect their body from painful or unwanted contact by the child.

20 of 190 posts.

These accounts range from hitting, biting, head-butting and thrown objects to accidental injuries during energetic play and unwanted comfort touching. Parents of two- and three-year-olds report broken noses, a concussion, bruises, painful climbing and being pursued after moving away; several say it happens mainly to one caregiver. Calm scripts, blocking, withdrawing, timeouts, sensory alternatives, spanking and behavioural therapy appear, without one shared outcome.

- "What has led me to reach out for help is the head butt I received yesterday at nap time that literally broke my nose. This kid has given me a black eye before…." [ar037-0021]
- "She hears "no", hits me, I say "I won't let you hit me" and move away. She then becomes even angrier, may throw things or scream for me, and sometimes asks for a hug." [ar065-0052]
- "I don’t want to yell. I don’t want to react out of fear. I don’t want to accidentally reinforce the behavior. But I also don’t want to be a punching bag." [ar071-0045]
- "He’s also in behavioral therapy but their ideas have also not worked." [ar047-0042]
- "It’s not because he’s angry, most of the time he’s playing or showing love." [ar062-0108]
- "He will try anything to get under my covers and put his hand in my shirt so he can rub it (even after telling him not to many times) and honestly it gives me the ick." [ar061-0134]
- "Now it seems like multiple times a day my son insists on trying to stand on them, kick off of them, use them as a hand hold for climbing." [ar054-0092]

**But:** One parent reports less throwing after four days of a belted timeout chair, and says the child rarely hits: "I do think since implementing time out chair the throwing has decreased a lot and it’s only been 4days." [ar058-0007]

Posts: 1sg1m9y 1shy5ko 1th8mif 1tpslyd 1u27tzj 1udqnt9 1umxg8s 1uusczz 1uz889p 1v32s25 1v7nr7g 1vc3x5t 1vt401p 1vyvglx 1w2ikfq 1w3zzpf 1w9hax0 1wc9up7 1wsq4hg 1wvaxg7

### The parent wants to stop the child hurting or ignoring the boundaries of other children.

7 of 190 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe biting, pushing, hitting or repeatedly touching other children, often around toys or frustration at daycare. Some behaviours occur only at school; others recur after a vacation or seem to follow rough play with a particular friend. One parent asks how to respond to newly touching other children's private parts, and another fears raising a boy who ignores physical boundaries.

- "Examples include constantly hugging peers, constantly touching their shoes or shirt if there is a cool design, nudging students out of the way if he wants something. Peers will tell him no and he persists." [ar026-0075]
- "My son is two years old. He recently started touching other kids private parts." [ar035-0138]
- "Today it was exceptionally bad she would seek her out when she wasn’t even anywhere near her and would find her to push her or grab her shirt and pull her down." [ar062-0028]
- "But man it’s just frustrating as a parent to do all the reading, role playing to ask for help and follow him around 24/7 when around other kids and have the same result everytime." [ar062-0063]
- "My family keeps telling me we need to bite her back but I just cannot bring myself to do that." [ar003-0076]

**But:** A follow-up reports that hitting friends stopped, although the child still hits himself or a toy when frustrated: "he sometimes either flaps his hands and hits himself or the toy he is playing with but has stopped hitting his friends.." [ar017-0015]

Posts: 1sehxap 1st58c6 1usrvke 1va1nob 1wvfzgs 1wwff34 1x3ecmn

### The parent wants adults to protect a child from repeated harm by other children.

5 of 190 posts.

Parents want a prevention plan, clearer incident reporting or a discussion with educators after repeated bites, blows or pushing involving their two- or three-year-old. Injuries include broken skin, a narrowly missed eye, a black eye and a bump from a spade; one parent connects a new aggressive personality and disturbed sleep with accounts of preschool harm. They often acknowledge ordinary toddler conflict while questioning its frequency or severity.

- "My 2yo son has gotten bit 2x this week, and probably 15 times in the past 3 months." [ar028-0083]
- "If it was biting, pushing etc. things that children often do, I’d be more understanding, but two blows to the head that have involved bruising and swelling is not okay in my opinion." [ar083-0024]
- "Transparency. We can assume the other parents have been notified, but we don't know ultimately." [ar041-0054]
- "She’s started sleep talking in the night and saying things like “go away” “stop it”." [ar020-0044]
- "we’re essentially paying a second mortgage for my daughter to get attacked." [ar082-0048]

**But:** The harm does not erase every positive account of the setting: "Aside from this, the daycare is amazing. First time we have encountered an issue." [ar041-0053]

Posts: 1svlzss 1sx1rzq 1ti5yy1 1tul8mv 1xcp64c

### The parent wants help with frequent or prolonged meltdowns and to know when they will ease.

23 of 190 posts.

Parents ask whether sudden or intensified meltdowns are ordinary development, how long they last and what will help in the moment. The children are two or three, with episodes after naps, illness, daycare, potty training, a move or the arrival of a sibling; some parents cannot identify a reliable trigger. They describe brief repeated fits as well as long evening or post-nap episodes, public exits, self-hitting and a fear that they are failing.

- "This past week, though - I don’t know, man, this is a completely different kid." [ar058-0015]
- "My son is 2.5 years old and every evening for the past two weeks he tantrums from around 6pm-when he goes to bed around 7:30/8:30pm." [ar063-0020]
- "She is absolutely inconsolable after waking from mid day nap. Doesn't want to go back to sleep, doesn't want cuddles. Rages and rages, pushing doors, pushing parents, stomping." [ar039-0002]
- "Is it the constipation, the potty training, just being 3, all of it? Did it end, how long did it take?" [ar078-0082]
- "Now I feel dread and anxiety. Am I failing? I truly don’t know what to do and it’s really effecting my mental health." [ar027-0058]
- "And it’s also scared me from going to places like this with her again. It was a very horrible experience." [ar013-0101]

**But:** There are effective comforts and recoveries within these accounts, including a blanket, TV after a nap and eating during a car ride: "The only thing that reliably works is her blankie. It regulates her and she calms down." [ar017-0057]

