### The parent cannot make sense of the child's contrary demands or distress over small frustrations.

8 of 190 posts.

Parents of two- and three-year-olds describe loops in which the child requests something, rejects it when offered, wants independence and then demands help. Other posts concern crying because a cousin said goodbye twice or becoming furious when blocks, tiles or figurines fall. Parents try choices, explanations, reassurance, deep breaths or daily outings and ask how to handle reactions they cannot resolve by granting the stated wish.

- "he will immediately say 'no, I want to eat!'. We'll then say 'okay, let's eat' and he'll jump back to 'no, I don't want to eat'." [ar007-0038]
- "Asks to do something then says help me we try to help and then says they want to do it themselves crying hysterically" [ar057-0058]
- "I said why are you crying and she said because she said bye two times!!!!" [ar045-0014]
- "Whether it’s his blocks falling, struggling to scoop up an ice cube, or his animal figurines tipping over. Every minor inconvenience seems to trigger a full-blown meltdown." [ar070-0176]
- "Sometimes if it's not important I just let it go, but it worries me when he insists he wants to do something dangerous or harmful." [ar007-0011]

**But:** The same parents also describe affectionate or manageable periods: "And then gives me a kiss exactly where it hurts. 🥹" [ar002-0193]

