### The parent cannot keep the child safe through words alone when the child runs, climbs or refuses restraints.

9 of 190 posts.

Parents cannot rely on explanations when a two- or three-year-old runs into traffic, leaves home, climbs furniture or refuses the stroller, hand-holding or car seat. Some want the child to practise independence safely; others need locks that fit unusual drawers or ways to stop climbing that defeats existing childproofing. One parent describes a short missing-child search ending safely, then installing a chain lock.

- "Just now I pulled her back from walking in front of a school bus." [ar019-0005]
- "He's running away from me, biting me, refusing to hold my hand and starts kicking and crying when I try to pick him up." [ar072-0016]
- "My friends who have not had trouble with the crib-to-toddler bed transition (i.e., all of my friends) all have one thing in common, which is that their children are not constantly climbing every fucking thing in the universe. Their children possess *fear.*" [ar064-0049]
- "i always wondered how these things happen but know i understand it only takes a second, literally." [ar058-0083]
- "Any suggestions or ideas to help him be the explorer he wants to be while making sure he is safe." [ar036-0099]
- "“Daddy, I never die”" [ar068-0095]

**But:** A parent's account includes initial learning before the unsafe behaviour began: "The first two days with the scooter, she was doing so well. She would stop as soon as she saw the street and wait, which was often 1 or 2 houses before the street." [ar019-0001]

Posts: 1sf8j8x 1sz10fb 1vd1t7t 1w2x1ky 1w5f29l 1w7h7n1 1wg34ed 1wh95cu 1x2sp7e

### The parent cannot get necessary body care done without resistance, distress or restraint.

14 of 190 posts.

Parents and relatives describe resistance to dressing, diaper changes, toothbrushing, bathing, hair care, nail trimming and sunscreen application in two- and three-year-olds. Attempts include choices, playful props, an electric toothbrush, preferred clothes, counting, bribes, washing quickly and physical restraint; some ask for a professional or worry about overriding bodily autonomy. The distress can be tightly tied to one task rather than a generally uncooperative child.

- "Every day that toothbrush comes out and the kid instantly covers his mouth with a death grip and forces his mouth shut." [ar056-0096]
- "He is laughing the whole time, but I'm trying my best to contain the rage after eating a few kicks.  He's strong, the kicks can hurt." [ar070-0034]
- "Is there somewhere I can take him that specializes in this type of thing? Like a groomer but for a kid (THAT sounds so bad)" [ar049-0005]
- "tried going fast and getting it over with, tried taking it slow and talking about it and using deep breathing, tried to use her dolls as a social story" [ar023-0107]
- "Since then it’s been a battle. Did I break him or something? Did I do the right thing? Help! I need more ideas 😩" [ar070-0140]
- "How do you suggest I go about it when I can’t respect his nos? How do I teach that no means no… most of the time?" [ar073-0135]

**But:** Resistance is sometimes specific to setting or task: "If I tell him we are getting a delivery, or if we are leaving the house he has no problem wearing them, but if we are just hanging out at home he insists on being naked from the waist down." [ar030-0002]

