### The parent describes a sudden or worsening difficult phase around age two or three.

15 of 189 posts.

Parents describe an apparent switch around a second or third birthday, or a new surge later in those years: a previously easier child now screams, refuses, hits or reacts intensely to small frustrations. Many ask whether this is normal, how long it lasts and whether they caused it, even when they believe the behaviour is developmentally expected. Contexts mentioned include potty training, disrupted naps, a new puppy and starting preschool; the share does not establish which caused the change.

- "She started her tantrums immediately after turning 2. Send help for all of us. I don't know what to do to help her. It's like the button for tantrums was clicked when she had her birthday." [ar003-0054]
- "My son just turned 3 last month and ever since it’s been a switch when it comes to meltdowns. If something doesn’t go his way, instant whining which leads to screaming and crying. All we can do is try to redirect, validate how he’s feeling and just let him at it until he calms down and we lose our hearing. I have an almost 5 year old and I remember when she turned 3, her tantrums became more intense. I just don’t remember when it got better…any advice?" [ar030-0001]
- "Up until my daughter turned 2 1/2 she was an easy going toddler who listened well and had minimal tantrums. Life was good and our home was full of love and fun energy. The last 2 months she’s suddenly become a true nightmare with zero explanation. I am losing my sanity and honestly don’t want to be around her sometimes because it’s always a fight with her." [ar068-0034]
- "I want to know if you had a similar kid, what are they like now, years later?  How long did this phase last and how did you get through it?" [ar032-0058]
- "I've tried staying calm, getting down to their level, offering hugs, giving them space, distracting them, and explaining things once they've settled, but nothing seems to make a consistent difference. Some days I feel like I'm walking on eggshells because I never know what's going to trigger the next meltdown." [ar019-0111]
- "Speaking sternly or putting my foot down always seems to just escalate things. My wife who has a lot more experience with kids is good at de-escalating, but without seeing improvement with his behaviour (worsening actually) I’m worried, you know I’m just worried to have a bad kid on my hands and it pains me to think of my own failure being the most likely cause." [ar056-0109]
- "[…] we got a puppy (she’s 8 months old) last week and since then, my daughter has just been absolutely wild. Coooooonstantly talking, hyper fixated on the dog, doesn’t listen to a single thing, just talks and talks and dances and sings and spins and babbles, she’s just so disregulated, and it’s making me resent her! My daughter not the dog. […]" [ar081-0061]
- "[…] after preschool which started about 3 months ago, everything changed!! […]" [ar011-0136]

**But:** Some parents describe the change with humour and do not experience every new protest as a personal rejection. "Yeah I definitely don't take it personally. I think she'd react the same way if I cried. She'd be devastated!" [ar070-0130]

