# baby-development: pattern 3, Hard behaviour (children of 2 and 3 years)

**Written:** 2026-11-20 from baby-development/evidence/2026-11/age-reread/digest-04.md baby-development/evidence/2026-11/age-reread/digest-05.md
**Reviewed by the owner:** not yet

## Limits

A random, keyword-free sample of 20,000 Reddit posts from twelve parenting subreddits, 1 April–24 October 2026, yielded 3,811 on-niche posts, including 1,340 about children of two and three. This pattern contains 379 yes and 41 near posts, all read; counts are posts tagged by one reader, with independently named forms merged by judgement, not market prevalence. Supplied classifications are preserved, including an age exception noted below; near posts include other ages and unknown ages.

## On-niche posts

### The parent cannot get cooperation through repeated rules and choices.

54 of 379 posts.

Contradictory demands and refusals turn daily routines into negotiations; granting the stated wish may not settle anything.

- "He is never following my rules without some whining, I have to repeat everything 10 times, and each time he makes up a distraction that he needs to do before proceeding." [ar003-0001]
- "he will immediately say 'no, I want to eat!'. We'll then say 'okay, let's eat' and he'll jump back to 'no, I don't want to eat'." [ar007-0038]

**But:** A choice can produce cleanup, though bedtime ran late. "We ​ were up a little late, but boy it looks tidy in here for a change!" [ar011-0106]

### The parent wants to prevent painful contact and harm to people or pets.

41 of 379 posts.

Unwanted soothing touch, rough affection and aggression can cause serious injuries; parents also fear animals retaliating.

- "What has led me to reach out for help is the head butt I received yesterday at nap time that literally broke my nose. This kid has given me a black eye before…." [ar037-0021]
- "My daughter seems to think she has to hold the kitten tighter so she won't escape, which obviously makes the situation worse." [ar005-0098]

**But:** Painful contact need not be angry. "It’s not because he’s angry, most of the time he’s playing or showing love." [ar062-0108]

### The parent wants to know why meltdowns intensified and when they ease.

38 of 379 posts.

Sudden switches and prolonged episodes occur around birthdays or family changes, without an established cause.

- "My son is 2.5 years old and every evening for the past two weeks he tantrums from around 6pm-when he goes to bed around 7:30/8:30pm." [ar063-0020]
- "I want to know if you had a similar kid, what are they like now, years later?  How long did this phase last and how did you get through it?" [ar032-0058]

**But:** Some find reliable comfort. "The only thing that reliably works is her blankie. It regulates her and she calms down." [ar017-0057]

### The parent struggles to complete care without distress or force.

34 of 379 posts.

Teeth, hair, nails, clothing, diapers and sometimes food become battles; parents question restraint and bodily autonomy.

- "Every day that toothbrush comes out and the kid instantly covers his mouth with a death grip and forces his mouth shut." [ar056-0096]
- "How do you suggest I go about it when I can’t respect his nos? How do I teach that no means no… most of the time?" [ar073-0135]

**But:** A particular product can matter. "[…] She LOVES brushing her teeth with the clear training toothpaste but won't even try colored toothpaste without a fight. […]" [ar082-0011]

### The parent wants siblings safe without rejecting the older child.

32 of 379 posts.

Rough affection, attacks and jealousy compete with another child’s needs; one account concerns an older stepsibling frightening a toddler.

- "Last night he was kicking his brother and when we gently told him no kicking, he head butted the baby." [ar025-0043]
- "Obviously in the long run I want him to learn how to be happy for others and not be sad when others are the center of attention and getting presents. However, he's two, he's already had a tough year." [ar026-0034]

**But:** Changes after birth need not target the baby. "ever since then she has gotten more aggressive towards us (not him)." [ar052-0050]

### The parent wants safe outings without chasing or physically removing the child.

28 of 379 posts.

Running, climbing, escapes and refused restraints threaten safety and ordinary family trips.

- "Just now I pulled her back from walking in front of a school bus." [ar019-0005]
- "[…] today my toddler refused to get in the car seat for one hour. one hour I sat at the daycare parking lot. […]" [ar058-0085]

**But:** Some settings remain manageable. "he is a great kid and extremely well behaved, no pulling throwing kicking none of that. He absolutely hates social gatherings. Infact when he is in a clean and less noisy set up he seems to do okay. And even when shopping." [ar002-0113]

### The parent wants to stop reacting angrily while keeping limits and affection.

24 of 379 posts.

Yelling, retaliation, guilt, resentment and regret coexist with love; one parent names temptation to return to substances.

- "I just don’t want to yell. I want to be his safe space." [ar052-0009]

**But:** An aunt reports calmer reactions. "But our home is much calmer now, and I feel far more in control of my reactions than I did before" [ar016-0048]

### The parent wants the child to respect peers and remain included.

21 of 379 posts.

Concerns include private-part touching, aggression, sharing disputes and childcare expulsion; home strategies may not transfer to school.

- "My son is two years old. He recently started touching other kids private parts." [ar035-0138]

**But:** Staff sometimes see a manageable issue. "Although the director reassured us that it isn’t anything major and they just want to get ahead of it, I’m mortified. […]" [ar068-0056]

### The parent wants relief from loud, rude or hurtful speech.

20 of 379 posts.

Screaming, whining, baby voices and copied insults or racial language bring pain, embarrassment and uncertainty about understanding.

- "His scream is so high pitched. It’s not just annoying but hurts our ears and his twin." [ar066-0063]

**But:** Words need not carry understood meanings. "He obviously has absolutely no idea what either word means. He’ll just randomly say them because I think he remembers the sound and knows it gets a reaction." [ar081-0128]

### The parent wants exact words and a repeatable response.

18 of 379 posts.

Parents question consequences, comfort and developmental readiness. Two writers promote their own resources; claimed benefits are not independent reviews.

- "Someone (okay instagram reels) told me that using If statements are like threatening your child. Is that what I’m doing?! I thought I was outlining the consequence." [ar055-0013]

**But:** A resource promoter describes partial change. "[…] Not instantly fixing anything, but he engages with it in a completely different way. […]" [ar004-0006]

### The parent wants other adults to change their response.

10 of 379 posts.

Partners disagree over discipline and comfort; relatives’ modelling and adults failing to prevent harm also concern parents.

- "He constantly tells me, in front of our kids, that my job is to be loving and nurturing and it's his job to be the disciplinarian." [ar047-0003]

**But:** Some accept another parent intervening. "When I parent their kid in front of them they never have a problem with it. So they know the behavior isn’t ok… I think?" [ar066-0077]

### The parent needs a break from constant activity and attention demands.

10 of 379 posts.

Movement, interruptions and rigid repeated games leave little room for another task or person.

- "I feel like I can't breathe sometimes because I get a single hour to myself during the day and he just can't let me have it." [ar004-0099]

**But:** Individual attention can change the encounter. "[…] I was able to spend a solo afternoon with him (he has a 10m old sister) and it was wonderful. He was sweet as pie. He wasn't climbing all over me and making crazy noise and being absolutely off the walls. We went on a hike and had ice cream and it was truly so great to be there with him. […]" [ar040-0073]

### The parent wants repetitive habits to stop damaging the child’s body or clothing.

7 of 379 posts.

Biting, picking, head-hitting and dismantled diapers appear. The supplied form includes a five-month-old despite this section’s age scope.

- "My 3 year old has started biting her nails and by that I mean she literally has like half of her nail bed left bc she bites the rest off." [ar057-0039]

**But:** One child tries an alternative. "She seems to like the idea and even saw her trying it at one point without me even asking 😲" [ar057-0047]

### The parent wants repeated licking, spitting and throwing to stop.

7 of 379 posts.

Mess during play and meals resists redirection; drinks and learning opportunities must remain available.

- "She licks anything and everything. Her arm, my arm, the light switch, the doorknob, the wall, shopping carts, toys, windows, she recently started licking her newborn sister’s head. if she’s in front of it: she will lick it." [ar016-0003]

**But:** The behaviour can be isolated. "Do you guys have any recommendations to discourage this behavior? Any advice on how to react? It’s driving me nuts! He’s a very easygoing and obedient kid otherwise." [ar069-0116]

### The parent wants professional help beyond their current tools.

6 of 379 posts.

Evaluations, reassurance or not qualifying for services leave some parents still seeking help.

- "I’ve already gotten him tested for things like Autism twice, but each time they say he’s not autistic, just very angry." [ar016-0019]

**But:** One hospitalized child is unusually aggressive. "He's never usually aggressive? He has been, especially with doctors, but never to this extent. I'm hoping it's just the sedation." [ar028-0045]

### The parent struggles when the child rejects one caregiver and demands another.

6 of 379 posts.

Preferred parents lose breaks; rejected parents feel hurt or ineffective.

- "But man, what a painful thing to have to listen to. Every plea for "mama" is like a cheese grater on my brain and nothing I can say helps. Gaaaaah! Sometimes being the preferred parent sucks." [ar022-0087]

**But:** A valued relationship can coexist with rejection. "[…] She is an insanely smart girl, is very observant and well-spoken, all around a wonderful kid." [ar081-0014]

### The parent wants adults to prevent repeated childcare injuries.

5 of 379 posts.

Parents seek prevention and clearer reporting after repeated bites or blows.

- "My 2yo son has gotten bit 2x this week, and probably 15 times in the past 3 months." [ar028-0083]

**But:** The setting may otherwise be valued. "Aside from this, the daycare is amazing. First time we have encountered an issue." [ar041-0053]

### The parent wants fewer screen demands after screens helped them cope.

5 of 379 posts.

Persistent requests and distress during limits or removal sometimes divide caregivers.

- "It's been 3 days since we took her Ipad away and....man I'm struggling, she is not reacting well to her Ipad being gone." [ar018-0061]

**But:** One reports improvement after reducing TV. "After school we divert his TV request to a list of other fun things we can do together. We think the tantrums are already getting better… but we’ll see." [ar035-0101]

### The parent wants comfort without painful touching or pacifier dependence.

4 of 379 posts.

Substitutes and removal can bring distress or continued skin picking.

- "Hey all I am seeking some desperate advice on how to get my girl to stop soothing / settling to sleep by digging into my knuckles, bending my fingers back and just fiddling with my hands." [ar062-0001]

**But:** Closeness is still welcome. "I would just absolutely love to hold and cuddle her but she just wants my damn hands and I NEED this to stop." [ar062-0005]

### The parent wants meals without constant prompting or destruction.

4 of 379 posts.

Sitting, food throwing and consequences make family meals exhausting.

- "No one in the family is relaxed during meals, the “come back for another bite” takes over any conversations and it’s at an infuriating point for everyone." [ar073-0052]

**But:** Throwing has several reported contexts. "This is usually done when he's unexcited about the food provided, he's bored/tired, wants attention, or is just simply finished eating his meal." [ar051-0098]

### The parent wants to reassure a child frightened of flooding after a cartoon.

1 of 379 posts.

Demonstrating overflow has not resolved the fear.

- "[…] My 3 year old is having full on panic attacks and freak outs every time she hears the sink, the toilet flush, bath tub, or hose turn on. […]" [ar061-0026]
- "She thinks if something overflows it will immediately flood everything and she will be underwater. I tried showing her that it just means the water gently goes over the side and that it wouldn’t cause a biblical flood haha" [ar061-0029]

**But:** nothing found against it.

### The parent wonders whether other families enjoy toddler companionship more.

1 of 379 posts.

An influencer’s account prompts comparison with daily protests and no help.

- "All of my mum friends have girls and it does make me feel like I’m doing consistently terrible" [ar018-0144]

**But:** The parent also enjoys motherhood. "[…] I don’t hate my motherhood. I’m a SAHM by choice, I love being a mum. Dare I even say it’s come pretty easy to me from the start and I don’t find it overly hard even though I have a hard child. […]" [ar018-0121]

### Other: the parent questions illness, a disputed hitting account or hidden supplies.

3 of 379 posts.

These are separate situations; illness suspicions and the disputed allegation remain the posters’ accounts.

